vkr: plain fences for the queues' fence syncs (WinBoat)

A fence on a Venus queue's timeline (virgl_renderer_context_create_fence with
a ring index) retires once the sync thread's VkFence signals. That fence was
created SYNC_FD-exportable, and NVIDIA's driver (615.78, RTX 3080 laptop) signals
such a fence up to 10 ms after the GPU work before it finished: an exportable
fence after a 4 MB copy waits 10 ms, a plain one 0.35 ms (on the host, no VM).
Nothing exports these fences (vkGetFenceFdKHR is for the guest's own fences),
so they are plain ones now. Mesa's Venus from 25.x on backs every VkFence with
such a fence: a Windows guest's Vulkan swapchain ran at 92 fps on a GPU idling.

--- a/src/venus/vkr_queue.c
+++ b/src/venus/vkr_queue.c
@@ -28,13 +28,11 @@
       if (!sync)
          return NULL;
 
-      const VkExportFenceCreateInfo export_info = {
-         .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_EXPORT_FENCE_CREATE_INFO,
-         .handleTypes = VK_EXTERNAL_FENCE_HANDLE_TYPE_SYNC_FD_BIT,
-      };
+      /* Not exportable: nothing exports these fences (the guest's own fences are separate), and NVIDIA's
+       * driver signals a SYNC_FD-exportable fence up to 10 ms after the work it waits for is done, where a
+       * plain fence takes a fraction of a millisecond (WinBoat) */
       const struct VkFenceCreateInfo create_info = {
          .sType = VK_STRUCTURE_TYPE_FENCE_CREATE_INFO,
-         .pNext = dev->physical_device->KHR_external_fence_fd ? &export_info : NULL,
       };
       VkResult result =
          vk->CreateFence(dev->base.handle.device, &create_info, NULL, &sync->fence);
